<section class="eg-page-hero"><div class="eg-container"><span class="eg-eyebrow">Your first workflow</span><h1>Run two nodes.<br>See the contract in action.</h1><p class="eg-lede">Collect a destination, save it as an accepted fact, and pass control to a second node that creates a trip card. Then inspect the saved session and run the validation tests.</p><div class="eg-actions"><a class="eg-button" href="/assets/ezgraph/ezgraph-starter.zip" download>Download the TypeScript starter <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="eg-button eg-button-secondary" href="#node-contract">Read the node code</a></div><p class="eg-note">Node.js 22.5 or later. No model credentials or EZGraph account needed for this scripted run.</p></div></section>
<nav class="eg-container eg-page-nav" aria-label="Starter steps"><a href="#run">1. Run</a><a href="#node-contract">2. Read the nodes</a><a href="#test">3. Test</a><a href="#next">4. Connect a provider</a></nav>
<section class="eg-article-section" id="run"><div class="eg-container eg-narrow"><div class="eg-step-head"><span>01</span><h2>Extract the project and run it.</h2></div><p>The download contains the package configuration, TypeScript settings, graph, entrypoint, tests, and README. Extract it, open a terminal in its parent directory, and run:</p><div class="eg-terminal"><div class="eg-terminal-label">Local terminal</div><pre><code>cd ezgraph-starter
npm install
npm start</code></pre></div><p>The entrypoint uses <code>scriptedGateway()</code> to provide predictable model replies. The real compiled graph, tool validation, handlers, histories, and session engine execute.</p><div class="eg-terminal eg-terminal-output"><div class="eg-terminal-label">Expected conversation output · followed by the complete session JSON</div><pre><code>You: Hi
EZGraph: Which city would you like to visit?
You: Lisbon
EZGraph: Trip to Lisbon: choose your dates, then book transport.
Completed: true

Session document:
{ … }</code></pre></div><div class="eg-callout"><strong>What this first run establishes</strong><p>The graph continues a session across two turns, saves accepted city data, enters the next node, and finishes with exact content from code. Scripted replies establish contract behavior; live model quality needs a separate evaluation. The memory session lasts for this process.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="eg-article-section" id="node-contract"><div class="eg-container eg-narrow"><div class="eg-step-head"><span>02</span><h2>Read the workflow from top to bottom.</h2></div><p><code>CityNode</code> publishes <code>capture_city</code> and saves valid input in its own channel. <code>go(SummaryNode)</code> enters the next stage in the same turn. That node reads the saved city, creates a trip card in code, and calls <code>finish()</code>.</p><p>The graph declares the model and named histories. Its topology registers the two conversational nodes and the built-in termination path.</p><div class="eg-code"><div class="eg-code-head"><span>workflow.ts · complete file</span><span>TypeScript</span></div><pre><code class="language-typescript">{{ ezgraphStarter["workflow.ts"] | escape }}</code></pre></div><p><a href="/assets/ezgraph/starter/workflow.ts" download>Download this file</a> · <a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/tool-responses/">Explore all response builders</a> · <a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/nodes-and-execution/">Read the lifecycle contract</a></p><h3>The entrypoint drives two real turns.</h3><div class="eg-code"><div class="eg-code-head"><span>main.ts · complete file</span><span>TypeScript</span></div><pre><code class="language-typescript">{{ ezgraphStarter["main.ts"] | escape }}</code></pre></div><p>The first turn returns a question and keeps <code>CityNode</code> active. The second captures Lisbon, enters <code>SummaryNode</code>, and completes. The printed document contains both node channels and the two history spaces.</p></div></section>
<section class="eg-article-section" id="test"><div class="eg-container eg-narrow"><div class="eg-step-head"><span>03</span><h2>Test the application boundary.</h2></div><p>Run <code>npm test</code>. The included tests exercise complete turns through <code>createTurnHarness()</code>, with an in-memory session store.</p><ul class="eg-check-list"><li>A second turn restores the active node and saves both channels.</li><li>A schema-invalid argument and a domain-invalid city are repaired before advancing.</li><li>The city stage offers <code>capture_city</code>; the summary stage offers <code>make_plan</code>.</li></ul><p>Change the city, the validation rule, or the code-owned trip card, then update the expected result. Inspect <code>gateway.calls</code> to see the actual tools offered by each stage.</p><a class="eg-link" href="/assets/ezgraph/starter/workflow.spec.ts" download>Download the test source <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></section>
<section class="eg-article-section" id="next"><div class="eg-container eg-narrow"><div class="eg-step-head"><span>04</span><h2>Connect a model and your application.</h2></div><p>Keep the node and tool contracts. Replace the scripted graph model with a catalog model for your provider, and replace the testing harness with <code>GraphEngine.create()</code> and its provider adapters. The developer guide shows the supported initialization contract.</p><div class="eg-fit"><div><h3>Choose model policy.</h3><p>Set graph defaults, node overrides, and recovery behavior. Configure your own provider credentials.</p><a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/models-and-error-handling/">Models and recovery →</a></div><div id="application-owned-database-initialization"><h3>Choose session storage.</h3><p>Compose selected database client factories in your application entrypoint. Configure ownership and shutdown with the engine.</p><a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/state-context-and-history/#application-owned-database-initialization">Application database initialization →</a></div></div><h3>Then try a complete application.</h3><p><a href="/ezgraph/quote-graph/">QuoteGraph</a> adds staged validation, catalog disambiguation, revisions, rating, and explicit acceptance. The <a href="/ezgraph/workflows/">workflow examples</a> show extraction and reviewed presentations using the same application layer.</p></div></section>
